    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "MENU\nAloha\nHAWAII'S GREATEST PAGEANT • October 30 to November 6, 1949\nWeek",
      "• It's the Mardi Gras, Frontier Days,\nand New Year's Eve-all packed\ninto one great week of thrilling entertainment\nyou'll never forget! During this\nspectacular Hawaiian holiday, or \"Makabiki,\nHonolulu shuns business attire to blossom forth in all the\ncolor and gaiety of a tropical rainbow.\n• Gardenias and flaming hibiscus appear in every\nhairdress. Fragrant plumeria and carnation leis encircle shoulders\nof visitor and Islander alike...and everywhere is\nthe brightly flowered boloku-the picturesque formal dress\nof early Hawaii, and the tapa-patterned \"aloba\" shirt.\n• Each day of Aloha Week presents authentic pageants depicting\nhighlights of Hawaiian history... the landing of\nKing Kamehameha I....installation ceremony for Hawaiian\nchieftains... ancient dancing and sporting events...and early village life.\nor Hawaiian a life\nEvenings are just as eventful. You'll\nattend hula pageants, native luaus (feasts),\nand dance under the stars at famous\nclubs and hotels. A holiday you'll talk about\nfor years to come!\nMENU\nThis menu prepared in United Air Lines Flight Kitchen\nat San Francisco. Frank Hurliman, Chef.\nCoffee\nCalifornia Fruit Cocktail au Curacao\nMilked Veal Cutlet\nSauce aux Fines Herbes\nEgg Noodles Polonaise\nCreamed New Peas\nPoppy Seed Roll\nSliced Tomato Salad\nLorenzo Dressing\nGateau au Mocha\nTea\nChocolate Mint\nMilk\nUNITED\nLINES\nTHE MAIN LINE BETWEEN THE MAINLAND AND HAWAII"
    ],
